\paragraph{Methodological implications.}
The decoupling of quality estimation ($\rho$), binary discrimination (AUROC), and sample selection (best-of-$N$ F1) means that evaluating confidence signals on a single metric risks selecting the wrong signal for the intended use case.
Threshold-based filtering favors logprob; ranking-dependent routing favors structural consistency; and effective best-of-$N$ selection likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers} that can evaluate individual outputs rather than distribution-level properties.

